  <div class="math-guide-key" role="note"><span><strong>Illustration:</strong> browser-only floating-point 2D/3D geometry</span><span><strong>Exact preview:</strong> supplied rational maps, not rechecked here</span><span><strong>Checked:</strong> native node replay with reported limits</span></div>
  <section class="surface panel-pad math-primer" aria-labelledby="mathPrimerTitle">
    <div class="panel-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">THREE IDEAS TO CARRY THROUGH THE JOURNEY</div><h2 id="mathPrimerTitle">What is an elliptic kernel?</h2></div></div>
    <p>A curve is a set of mathematical points. An <em>isogeny kernel</em> is a finite subgroup of points sent to the identity by a group-preserving map between elliptic curves. That is different from the <em>Fourier kernel</em> (complex rotating basis functions) used to draw the image below.</p>
    <div class="math-primer-grid">
      <article><span class="math-concept-symbol">E / P</span><h3>Start with a curve and a point</h3><p>The animation samples a real curve, E(ℝ). Native pathfinding instead represents exact rational curves and points over ℚ, with no rounding of coefficients.</p><a href="https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/arithmetic_curves/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Elliptic curves · SageMath ↗</a></article>
      <article><span class="math-concept-symbol">φ / ker φ</span><h3>Move between curves</h3><p>An isogeny φ: E → E′ is an algebraic group-preserving map; its kernel specifies which source points map to the identity. ECAI searches bounded degree-2 kernels and verifies the resulting rational maps.</p><a href="https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/arithmetic_curves/sage/schemes/elliptic_curves/ell_curve_isogeny.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Isogenies and kernels · SageMath ↗</a></article>
      <article><span class="math-concept-symbol">✓ / ?</span><h3>Separate proof from search</h3><p>Polynomial identities can be replayed exactly for supplied maps. A bounded search finds paths within its declared limits; not finding a path does not prove none exists.</p><button type="button" class="math-text-jump" data-math-jump="mathResults">See the evidence workbench ↘</button></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="surface panel-pad math-kernel" id="mathKernel" aria-labelledby="mathKernelTitle">
    <div class="panel-heading math-kernel-heading">
      <div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 01 · GET AN INTUITION · PUBLIC / BROWSER ONLY</div><h2 id="mathKernelTitle">Watch a curve become an image</h2></div>
      <span class="status-tag">Public · runs locally</span>
    </div>
    <p class="context-hint math-kernel-intro">A real point moves along <strong>y² = x³ − 4x</strong>. Its oval is reshaped into wings or petals; a finite Fourier series draws that contour one rotating vector at a time. Try the original oval first, then the dragonfly. Increase <strong>Fourier order</strong> to sharpen the approximation. This stage is visual intuition, not an isogeny or semantic proof.</p>
    <div class="math-kernel-visual">
      <div class="math-kernel-stage-heading">
        <span>Rotating Fourier vectors → reconstructed image</span>
        <div class="math-kernel-legend" aria-hidden="true"><span><i></i> Trace</span><span><i></i> Vectors</span></div>
      </div>
      <canvas id="mathKernelStage" role="img" aria-label="Fourier epicycles progressively reconstructing a shape derived from a real elliptic-curve oval" width="1100" height="550"></canvas>
      <div class="math-kernel-controls">
        <div class="math-kernel-buttons">
          <button type="button" class="button button-primary" id="mathKernelPlay" aria-pressed="false">Play</button>
          <button type="button" class="button button-outline" id="mathKernelReset">↺ Reset</button>
        </div>
        <div class="math-kernel-control"><label for="mathKernelTerms">Fourier order <output id="mathKernelTermsLabel" for="mathKernelTerms">12</output></label>
          <input id="mathKernelTerms" type="range" min="1" max="80" step="1" value="12">
          <label class="math-kernel-check"><input id="mathKernelAuto" type="checkbox" checked> Refine after each trace</label></div>
        <div class="math-kernel-control"><label for="mathKernelSpeed">Trace speed <output id="mathKernelSpeedLabel" for="mathKernelSpeed">1×</output></label>
          <input id="mathKernelSpeed" type="range" min="0.25" max="4" step="0.25" value="1"></div>
        <div class="math-kernel-control"><label for="mathKernelShape">Generated shape</label>
          <select id="mathKernelShape"><option value="dragonfly">Dragonfly · curve-derived wings</option><option value="flower">Six-petal flower</option><option value="oval">Original real oval</option></select></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="math-kernel-details">
      <article class="math-kernel-detail" aria-labelledby="mathKernelCurveTitle"><h3 id="mathKernelCurveTitle">01 / Weierstrass source</h3>
        <p class="math-kernel-equation">E : y² = x³ − 4x</p>
        <canvas id="mathKernelCurve" role="img" aria-label="Real bounded component of the elliptic curve with its moving point" width="340" height="140"></canvas>
        <p class="math-kernel-fine"><span id="mathKernelPoint">P = (0.00, 0.00)</span> · Real curve point</p></article>
      <article class="math-kernel-detail" aria-labelledby="mathKernelFourierTitle"><h3 id="mathKernelFourierTitle">02 / Fourier spectrum</h3>
        <p class="math-kernel-equation">z<sub>N</sub>(t) = Σ c<sub>k</sub>e<sup>2πikt</sup></p>
        <canvas id="mathKernelSpectrum" role="img" aria-label="Magnitudes of Fourier coefficients, showing the current included harmonics" width="340" height="140"></canvas>
        <p class="math-kernel-fine">Finite sampling → complex coefficients → continuous-time reconstruction</p></article>
      <article class="math-kernel-detail" aria-labelledby="mathKernelMapTitle"><h3 id="mathKernelMapTitle">03 / Where the paths diverge</h3>
        <pre class="math-kernel-map">P ∈ E(ℝ)
↓ affine drawing transform
contour z(t)
↓ discrete Fourier transform
Σ cₖ exp(2πikt)
↓ continuous trace
image</pre>
        <p class="math-kernel-fine">An actual degree-2 isogeny with kernel {O, (0,0)} maps E to E′: y² = x³ + 16x using x′ = x − 4/x and y′ = y(1 + 4/x²) for x ≠ 0. That algebraic map is <em>not</em> the Fourier renderer.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="context-hint math-kernel-disclaimer">The wings use affine transformations of sampled elliptic-curve points; the dragonfly body is an illustrative primitive. Fourier coefficients reconstruct the <em>drawing</em>, not a new curve or a semantic relationship. The exact isogenies later in this guide are separate computations.</p>
    <div class="math-stage-next"><span><strong>Keep exploring the geometry.</strong> Move from a flat contour to a 3D mesh before starting the exact-algebra workbench. <a href="https://www.3blue1brown.com/lessons/fourier-series/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How rotating vectors draw shapes ↗</a></span><button type="button" class="button button-primary button-sm" data-math-jump="math3DWorkbench">Next: explore 3D geometry →</button></div>
  </section>
  <section class="surface panel-pad math-3d" id="math3DWorkbench" aria-labelledby="math3DTitle">
    <div class="panel-heading">
      <div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 01B · EXPERIMENT WITH 3D GEOMETRY · LOCAL / NO WALLET</div><h2 id="math3DTitle">Geometric experiment workbench</h2></div>
      <span class="status-tag">Deterministic visual model</span>
    </div>
    <p class="math-stage-description">Go beyond the flat Fourier trace. Sample the real Weierstrass curve <strong>E: y² = x³ − 4x</strong>, lift its geometry into a triangular 3D mesh, and orbit the resulting object. The dragonfly wings have a curve-derived profile; the torus follows a deformed oval and the helix uses the oval to modulate its radius. Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom, or use arrow keys with the canvas focused.</p>
    <div class="math-3d-layout">
      <div class="math-3d-viewport">
        <canvas id="math3DStage" width="1100" height="710" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Three-dimensional orbital viewer showing a procedural object derived from elliptic-curve samples"></canvas>
        <div class="math-3d-stats"><span><strong id="math3DVertices">—</strong> vertices</span><span><strong id="math3DFaces">—</strong> triangles</span><span>Source: E(ℝ)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="math-3d-controls">
        <label for="math3DShape">Object to reconstruct</label>
        <select id="math3DShape"><option value="dragonfly">3D dragonfly · curved wings</option><option value="elliptic-torus">Elliptic torus · surface + braid</option><option value="double-helix">Double helix · paired strands</option></select>
        <label for="math3DDetail">Mesh detail <output id="math3DDetailLabel" for="math3DDetail">22</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="math3DDetail" min="10" max="40" step="2" value="22">
        <label for="math3DTwist">Lift / twist <output id="math3DTwistLabel" for="math3DTwist">1</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="math3DTwist" min="0" max="5" step="0.5" value="1">
        <label class="math-3d-inline-check"><input type="checkbox" id="math3DEdges" checked> Show triangle edges</label>
        <div class="math-3d-actions"><button type="button" class="button button-outline button-sm" id="math3DPlay">Auto rotate</button><button type="button" class="button button-outline button-sm" id="math3DReset">Reset camera</button></div>
        <div class="math-3d-experiment"><strong>Preserve this experiment</strong><p>Make an OBJ mesh, a deterministic parameter/provenance manifest, a preview PNG and an AEX-141 metadata template.</p>
          <button type="button" class="button button-primary" id="math3DPrepare">Prepare experiment for mint →</button>
          <p class="math-3d-status" id="math3DStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite">Local geometry preview · not minted</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="math-3d-receipt" id="math3DReceipt" hidden>
      <h3>Experiment artifact · prepared locally</h3>
      <p>A reproducible geometry claim is ready to export. <strong>No IPFS upload, payment, signature or blockchain transaction has occurred.</strong></p>
      <dl><dt>Experiment SHA-256</dt><dd id="math3DExperimentId"></dd><dt>Mesh SHA-256</dt><dd id="math3DMeshHash"></dd></dl>
      <div class="math-3d-export-actions">
        <button type="button" class="button button-outline button-sm" id="math3DExportOBJ">Download 3D mesh (.obj)</button>
        <button type="button" class="button button-outline button-sm" id="math3DExportJSON">Download experiment (.json)</button>
        <button type="button" class="button button-outline button-sm" id="math3DExportPNG">Save preview (.png)</button>
        <button type="button" class="button button-outline button-sm" id="math3DExportNFT">AEX-141 metadata template</button>
      </div>
      <p class="math-3d-mint-note"><strong>Mint handoff:</strong> Pin the OBJ and the experiment manifest to IPFS. Replace the placeholder CIDs in the exported metadata, pin that metadata, and mint through an authorized AEX-141 contract using a wallet. Its <code>mint</code> extension and metadata type must match the deployed collection. ECAI's current index-artifact mint-review API is <em>not</em> an experiment minting API. <a href="https://docs.aeternity.com/developer-documentation/aexs/aexs/aex-141" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AEX-141 spec ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/aeternity/aex141-nft-collection-example" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reference contract / viewer ↗</a></p>
    </div>
    <p class="context-hint">What is (and is not) verified: the mathematical source points are sampled in floating point; model surfaces are deterministic parametric lifts. A geometry hash lets others compare the exact exported mesh bytes. It does <em>not</em> establish an algebraic isogeny, a proof of geometric uniqueness, a semantic equivalence or a DamageBDD execution report. Visit the exact pathfinder below for those distinct questions.</p>
    <div class="math-stage-next"><span><strong>From 3D geometry to algebraic evidence.</strong> See the distinction between a visual transformation and a rational map on elliptic curves. <a href="https://threejs.org/docs/pages/OrbitControls" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3D orbital controls ↗</a></span><button type="button" class="button button-primary button-sm" data-math-jump="mathLibrary">Next: inspect an exact example →</button></div>
  </section>
  <section class="surface panel-pad math-library" id="mathLibrary" aria-labelledby="mathLibraryTitle">
    <div class="panel-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">STEP 02 · INVESTIGATE AN EXACT CANDIDATE · PUBLIC</div><h2 id="mathLibraryTitle">Choose a problem to investigate</h2></div><span class="status-tag math-status-preview">Public sample library</span></div>
    <p class="math-stage-description">Every example names a source curve, a proposed path of rational maps, and a mathematical question. Selecting one loads a <strong>local, unverified-on-this-node preview</strong>; it does not run a node job. Try the default example, select arrows in the path below, or pick a deliberately invalid map to see what can fail.</p>
    <div class="math-sample-toolbar">
      <label for="mathSampleSelect">Choose a mathematical problem<select id="mathSampleSelect" aria-label="Choose a mathematical sample"></select></label>
