  <details class="surface panel-pad math-builder" id="mathBuilderDisclosure"><summary id="mathBuildSummary"><span class="math-step-kicker">STEP 03 · BOUNDED SEARCH · MANAGED SIGN-IN TO RUN</span><strong>Find a new transformation, or replay a supplied path</strong><span class="math-summary-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span></summary>
  <section aria-labelledby="mathBuildTitle">
    <div class="panel-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">RATIONAL ELLIPTIC CURVES · EXACT ARITHMETIC</div><h2 id="mathBuildTitle">Find a connection over ℚ</h2></div><button type="button" id="mathDemo" class="button button-outline button-sm" disabled>Run explicit demo</button></div>
    <p class="math-stage-description">Choose <strong>Explore from source</strong> to enumerate reachable curve models, or <strong>Connect two curves</strong> to search for a checked path between endpoints. The Erlang pathfinder uses exact rational arithmetic and bounded candidate degree-2 kernels. The outcome is evidence within the chosen search limits—not a complete classification.</p>
    <form id="mathDiscoverForm" class="stack-form">
      <div class="math-mode-row"><label>Question<select id="mathMode" name="operation"><option value="explore" selected>Explore from source · no target</option><option value="discover">Connect two given curves</option></select></label><p id="mathQuestionHint" class="context-hint">Which models can we reach, and which are non-isomorphic to the source over Q?</p></div>
      <div class="math-curve-inputs">
        <fieldset><legend>Source · y² = x³ + a₂x² + a₄x + a₆</legend><div class="math-coefficients">
          <label>a₂<input name="source_a2" value="0" maxlength="130" required aria-label="Source a2"></label>
          <label>a₄<input name="source_a4" value="-1" maxlength="130" required aria-label="Source a4"></label>
          <label>a₆<input name="source_a6" value="0" maxlength="130" required aria-label="Source a6"></label>
        </div></fieldset>
        <fieldset id="mathTargetFields" hidden disabled><legend>Target · y² = x³ + a₂x² + a₄x + a₆</legend><div class="math-coefficients">
          <label>a₂<input name="target_a2" value="0" maxlength="130" required aria-label="Target a2"></label>
          <label>a₄<input name="target_a4" value="-16" maxlength="130" required aria-label="Target a4"></label>
          <label>a₆<input name="target_a6" value="0" maxlength="130" required aria-label="Target a6"></label>
        </div></fieldset>
      </div>
      <div class="math-bounds">
        <label>Max hops<input name="max_depth" type="number" min="0" max="3" value="2" required></label>
        <label>Max curve models<input name="max_curves" type="number" min="1" max="32" value="24" required></label>
        <label>Kernel numerator |n| ≤<input name="numerator_bound" type="number" min="0" max="32" value="8" required></label>
        <label>Kernel denominator d ≤<input name="denominator_bound" type="number" min="1" max="16" value="4" required></label>
        <label id="mathTimeBudget">Search budget (ms)<input name="timeout_ms" type="number" min="1" max="20000" value="8000" required></label>
        <button type="submit" id="mathDiscover" class="button button-primary" disabled>Explore from source →</button>
      </div>
      <p class="context-hint">Enter integers or fractions, such as -1 or 3/5; no floating-point coefficients. Discovery considers bounded rational degree-2 kernels, not every possible isogeny.</p>
    </form>
    <div class="math-import-row"><label class="math-file-label" for="mathImportFile">Replay an existing path JSON<input id="mathImportFile" type="file" accept=".json,application/json" disabled></label><button type="button" id="mathImport" class="button button-outline button-sm" disabled>Import &amp; check</button><span class="context-hint">Path files only · up to 192 KiB · submitted flags are not trusted</span></div>
    <div class="math-stage-next"><span><strong>Already have a path?</strong> Import it for a fresh native check, then compare the transformed objects, rational points and algebraic identities below. <a href="https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/arithmetic_curves/sage/schemes/elliptic_curves/ell_curve_isogeny.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Learn about rational isogeny maps ↗</a></span><button type="button" class="button button-primary button-sm" data-math-jump="mathResults">Next: inspect &amp; verify →</button></div>
  </section></details>
